Extension API: the two turn-start research dialogs report synthetic screen
ids — ON THE WIRE ONLY.

STATUS: applied locally to ~/orion2re (engine 1.60.0, branch
orionlayer-local, commit f838c754) by work order 129 B, 17 September 2026,
Data's decision. Required by tools/version_check.py. It comes back off with

    cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
        ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_research_screens.patch

WHAT IT IS FOR

    At turn start, when a project completes, the game runs two dialogs in
    a row: SCIENCE::Science_Room_ (the presentation — scientist, device,
    description) and then TECH::_Tech_Select_(0), SELECT NEW RESEARCH.
    REPORT::Reports_Screen_ has set MOX::_current_screen = SCREEN_MAIN
    (mainscr2.cpp:119) and neither dialog writes it, so both report 0.
    OrionLayer therefore kept drawing the galaxy map, with an active TURN
    button, over dialogs the player has to answer in the game's own
    window — and a field sent into the select list crashes the engine
    (doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md item 23).

WHY NOT THE PATH open fix 22 TAKES

    doc/ext_screen_id.patch reports race selection's synthetic id by
    WRITING MOX::_current_screen. Here that would change the picture:
    the description box reads the same variable for its x — 84 on the
    main screen, 130 otherwise (textbox.cpp:40-50) — and again for its
    colour group (textbox.cpp:284). A patch that moved a dialog while
    making it visible would be trading one wrong picture for another.

    So this one adds `ext::g_screen_override`, which only
    `ext::Tick` reads when it serializes: the id SENT changes, the id the
    game dispatches and draws from does not. `ext::ScreenOverride` sets it
    for a scope and restores it, which is what a function with several
    returns needs (TECH::_Tech_Select_ has three).

THE IDS

    52  the science room. It is also the dialog for stolen and artifact
        technology (report.cpp), which is the same dialog and reports the
        same id; `research_breakthrough != 0` is what tells the research
        case apart, and it is on the wire.
    53  SELECT NEW RESEARCH, and only in select mode. CHANGE mode is
        Screen_Control_'s SCREEN_TECH_CHANGE (36) and keeps reporting it;
        the guard is constructed with -1 there, which does nothing.

    Both are past the SCREEN enum's last value, 43
    (SCREEN_SHOW_COMMAND_POINTS, orion2_consts.h), and beside the two
    synthetic ids already in use, 50 (Custom Race) and 51 (race
    selection). The smoke test holds that rule for every id in the tree.

    doc/newtech_reading.md is the reading this rests on.

THE DIFF

--- a/src/ext/ext_api.h
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.h
@@ -24,6 +24,26 @@
 /// Set by ProcessInput(), consumed by Get_Input_() early return.
 extern int16_t g_pending_field;
 
+/// The screen id REPORTED to clients, or -1 for the game's own.
+///
+/// OrionLayer needs the two turn-start research dialogs — the science
+/// room and SELECT NEW RESEARCH — to be distinguishable on the wire; both
+/// run under SCREEN_MAIN and the game draws them from that value, so
+/// writing MOX::_current_screen (the path the screen-id patch takes for
+/// race selection) would MOVE them: textbox.cpp:44 picks the description
+/// box's x from it and textbox.cpp:284 its colour group. This changes what
+/// is SENT and nothing the game reads.
+extern int16_t g_screen_override;
+
+/// Sets `g_screen_override` while it lives and restores the previous value
+/// when it goes out of scope — so a function with several returns needs one
+/// line. An id below 0 means "no override" and the guard does nothing.
+struct ScreenOverride {
+    int16_t previous;
+    explicit ScreenOverride(int16_t id);
+    ~ScreenOverride();
+};
+
 /// True while an injected mouse event is queued and not yet
 /// pumped. Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_ stands aside while this is
 /// set, so the injected position survives to whoever reads
--- a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
@@ -636,7 +636,25 @@
     return ok;
 }
 
+int16_t g_screen_override = -1;
+
+ScreenOverride::ScreenOverride(int16_t id) : previous(g_screen_override) {
+    if (id >= 0) {
+        g_screen_override = id;
+    }
+}
+
+ScreenOverride::~ScreenOverride() {
+    g_screen_override = previous;
+}
+
 void Tick(int16_t current_screen) {
+    // What the client is told. The override is the only difference; the
+    // game's own MOX::_current_screen is untouched, because the dialogs it
+    // distinguishes draw themselves from it (ext_api.h).
+    if (g_screen_override >= 0) {
+        current_screen = g_screen_override;
+    }
     // Accept new connections
     g_server.AcceptClients();
 
